					<div id="mw-content-text" class="mw-body-content"><div class="mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang="sco" dir="ltr"><p><b>Organic chemistry</b> is a <a href="/wiki/Chemistry" class="mw-redirect" title="Chemistry">chemistry</a> subdiscipline involvin the <a href="/wiki/Science" title="Science">scientific</a> study o the structur, properties, an reactions of <a href="/wiki/Organic_compoonds" class="mw-redirect" title="Organic compoonds">organic compoonds</a> an <a href="/w/index.php?title=Organic_materials&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1" class="new" title="Organic materials (page disna exeest)">organic materials</a>, i.e., matter in its sindry forms that contain <a href="/wiki/Carbon" class="mw-redirect" title="Carbon">carbon</a> <a href="/wiki/Atom" title="Atom">atoms</a>.<sup id="cite_ref-1" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-1"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>1<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id="cite_ref-J._Clayden,_N._Greeves_2012_pp._1-15_2-0" class="reference"><a href="#cite_note-J._Clayden,_N._Greeves_2012_pp._1-15-2"><span class="cite-bracket">&#91;</span>2<span class="cite-bracket">&#93;</span></a></sup>
